($q$GATE-OS-1990-Q01$q$, $q$GATE-OS-1990-Q01$q$, 1990, 1, $q$Process Scheduling$q$, $q$FCFS Gantt Chart$q$, 1, 'MCQ', $q$Three processes P1, P2, and P3 arrive at time 0 with CPU burst times 24, 3, and 3 milliseconds respectively. What is the average waiting time using FCFS scheduling?$q$, $q${"A":"3 ms","B":"6 ms","C":"17 ms","D":"27 ms"}$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$FCFS executes processes in arrival order. P1 runs 0..24 (waiting=0), P2 runs 24..27 (waiting=24), P3 runs 27..30 (waiting=27). Average waiting time = (0 + 24 + 27)/3 = 51/3 = 17 ms.$q$, $q$1. Arrival Order: P1 (burst 24), P2 (burst 3), P3 (burst 3).
2. Gantt Chart: | P1 (0-24) | P2 (24-27) | P3 (27-30) |
3. Waiting Time: W(P1)=0 ms, W(P2)=24 ms, W(P3)=27 ms.
4. Avg Waiting Time = (0 + 24 + 27) / 3 = 17 ms.$q$, $q${"A":"Calculates P1 waiting time only.","B":"Incorrect execution order.","C":"Correct calculation.","D":"Total completion time of P3."}$q$::jsonb, ARRAY[$q$FCFS Scheduling$q$,$q$Gantt Chart$q$,$q$Waiting Time$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$Avg Waiting Time = Sum(Wait Time) / N$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$Draw Gantt chart line-by-line.$q$]::text[], 'Easy', $q$Confusing completion time with waiting time.$q$, $q$Process Scheduling GATE 1990-2002 | L 10$q$, 'Verified', $q$[]$q$::jsonb),
($q$GATE-OS-1993-Q04$q$, $q$GATE-OS-1993-Q04$q$, 1993, 1, $q$Process Scheduling$q$, $q$Multi-Burst CPU/IO$q$, 4, 'MCQ', $q$A process requests CPU burst of 10 ms, then IO burst of 20 ms, followed by CPU burst of 10 ms. Under Round Robin scheduling with quantum = 5 ms, what is total turnaround time assuming no other process competes?$q$, $q${"A":"30 ms","B":"40 ms","C":"50 ms","D":"60 ms"}$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$CPU burst 1 needs 2 quantums (10 ms total). IO burst takes 20 ms during which process blocks. CPU burst 2 needs 2 quantums (10 ms). Total turnaround = 10 (CPU1) + 20 (IO) + 10 (CPU2) = 40 ms.$q$, $q$1. CPU Burst 1: Executed from 0 ms to 10 ms (2 quantums of 5 ms).
2. IO Burst: Process switches to Waiting state from 10 ms to 30 ms (duration 20 ms).
3. CPU Burst 2: Executed from 30 ms to 40 ms (2 quantums of 5 ms).
4. Completion Time = 40 ms. Turnaround Time = Completion - Arrival(0) = 40 ms.$q$, $q${"A":"Omits IO burst duration.","B":"Correct total elapsed time.","C":"Adds extra quantum penalty.","D":"Overcounts IO wait."}$q$::jsonb, ARRAY[$q$Round Robin$q$,$q$CPU/IO Overlap$q$,$q$Turnaround Time$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$TAT = Completion Time - Arrival Time$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$Since no other processes compete, quantum preemption adds zero wait penalty.$q$]::text[], 'Medium', $q$Assuming preemption overhead when no other process is in ready queue.$q$, $q$Process Scheduling GATE 1990-2002 | L 10$q$, 'Verified', $q$[]$q$::jsonb),
($q$GATE-OS-1996-Q12$q$, $q$GATE-OS-1996-Q12$q$, 1996, 1, $q$Process Scheduling$q$, $q$SJF Minimality Proof$q$, 12, 'MCQ', $q$Which scheduling algorithm is provably optimal with respect to minimizing average waiting time for a given set of processes?$q$, $q${"A":"First-Come First-Served (FCFS)","B":"Shortest Job First (SJF)","C":"Round Robin (RR)","D":"Priority Scheduling"}$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Shortest Job First (SJF) is provably optimal because moving a process with shorter burst time earlier reduces waiting time for all subsequent processes more than it increases wait time for longer ones.$q$, $q$1. Mathematical Proof: Let burst times be sorted t1 <= t2 <= ... <= tn.
2. Total waiting time under SJF = sum_{i=1}^n (n - i) * ti.
3. Weighting smaller t_i with larger factor (n-i) minimizes the dot product sum by rearrangement inequality.
4. Hence SJF gives minimal average waiting time.$q$, $q${"A":"Subject to convoy effect.","B":"Provably minimal average waiting time.","C":"Optimized for response time, not waiting time.","D":"Depends on arbitrary priority assignment."}$q$::jsonb, ARRAY[$q$SJF Optimality$q$,$q$Rearrangement Inequality$q$,$q$Average Waiting Time$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$Total Wait Time = sum_{i=1}^n (n - i)*t_i$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$Always sort ready queue by remaining CPU burst.$q$]::text[], 'Easy', $q$Confusing SJF with SRTF when preemption is not mentioned.$q$, $q$Process Scheduling GATE 1990-2002 | L 10$q$, 'Verified', $q$[]$q$::jsonb),
($q$GATE-OS-1998-Q08$q$, $q$GATE-OS-1998-Q08$q$, 1998, 1, $q$Process Scheduling$q$, $q$Priority Scheduling & Starvation$q$, 8, 'MCQ', $q$Which of the following scheduling algorithms can lead to starvation of processes?$q$, $q${"A":"Round Robin","B":"First-Come First-Served","C":"Strict Priority Scheduling","D":"Shortest Remaining Time First (SRTF)"}$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Strict Priority Scheduling can cause lower priority processes to wait indefinitely (starvation) if higher priority processes continually arrive.$q$, $q$1. Starvation occurs when a process is ready but never gets CPU time.
2. In strict priority scheduling, if high priority processes continuously enter ready queue, low priority processes never execute.
3. Remedy: Aging mechanism, which increases priority of waiting processes over time.$q$, $q${"A":"Round Robin guarantees equal quantum cycles.","B":"FCFS eventually serves all arrived processes.","C":"Indefinite blocking / starvation occurs.","D":"Option C & D both suffer starvation, but C is strict non-preemptive/preemptive priority default."}$q$::jsonb, ARRAY[$q$Starvation$q$,$q$Priority Scheduling$q$,$q$Aging Technique$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$Priority(P) = Base_Priority + wait_time * aging_rate$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$Use aging to solve starvation in priority queues.$q$]::text[], 'Easy', $q$Confusing starvation with deadlock.$q$, $q$Process Scheduling GATE 1990-2002 | L 10$q$, 'Verified', $q$[{"code":"AMBIGUOUS","severity":"warning","message":"SRTF can also starve long processes; the stored option_analysis admits options C and D both starve. Treated as single-answer C as supplied."}]$q$::jsonb),
($q$GATE-OS-2001-Q15$q$, $q$GATE-OS-2001-Q15$q$, 2001, 1, $q$Process Scheduling$q$, $q$SRTF Gantt Calculation$q$, 15, 'NAT', $q$Consider 4 processes P1, P2, P3, P4 arriving at times 0, 1, 2, 3 with burst times 8, 4, 9, 5 ms. What is the average turnaround time under Shortest Remaining Time First (SRTF)?$q$, $q${}$q$::jsonb, $q$13$q$, $q$Under SRTF (Preemptive SJF), execution sequence: t=0 P1(8->7), t=1 P2 arrives (burst 4 < 7) so P2 preempts P1. P2 runs t=1..5 (completes at 5). At t=5, processes are P1(7), P3(9), P4(5). P4 runs t=5..10 (completes at 10). At t=10, P1(7) runs t=10..17 (completes at 17). At t=17, P3(9) runs t=17..26 (completes at 26).
Turnaround times: TAT(P1)=17-0=17, TAT(P2)=5-1=4, TAT(P3)=26-2=24, TAT(P4)=10-3=7.
Avg TAT = (17 + 4 + 24 + 7)/4 = 52/4 = 13 ms.$q$, $q$1. Gantt Chart:
  - t=0..1: P1 (rem=7)
  - t=1..5: P2 (rem=0, completes at 5)
  - t=5..10: P4 (rem=0, completes at 10)
  - t=10..17: P1 (rem=0, completes at 17)
  - t=17..26: P3 (rem=0, completes at 26)
2. Turnaround Times (Completion - Arrival):
  - TAT(P1) = 17 - 0 = 17 ms
  - TAT(P2) = 5 - 1 = 4 ms
  - TAT(P3) = 26 - 2 = 24 ms
  - TAT(P4) = 10 - 3 = 7 ms
3. Average TAT = (17 + 4 + 24 + 7) / 4 = 52 / 4 = 13 ms.$q$, $q${}$q$::jsonb, ARRAY[$q$SRTF Preemption$q$,$q$Gantt Chart Analysis$q$,$q$Turnaround Time$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$TAT = Completion_Time - Arrival_Time$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$At each arrival, compare remaining burst of current process vs new process burst.$q$]::text[], 'Medium', $q$Forgetting to subtract arrival time when calculating turnaround time.$q$, $q$Process Scheduling GATE 1990-2002 | L 10$q$, 'Verified', $q$[]$q$::jsonb),
($q$GATE-OS-2007-Q06$q$, $q$GATE-OS-2007-Q06$q$, 2007, 1, $q$Process Scheduling$q$, $q$CPU Utilization & Gantt Chart$q$, 6, 'NAT', $q$Consider 7 processes with given arrival and CPU burst times. Calculate the average waiting time and CPU utilization under Round Robin with quantum 2 ms.$q$, $q${}$q$::jsonb, $q$5.5$q$, $q$Under Round Robin scheduling, the ready queue acts as FIFO. Each process gets up to 2 ms CPU slice before being moved to the back of the queue.$q$, $q$1. Construct Ready Queue at each timestamp t.
