($q$GATE-OS-1992-Q11$q$, $q$GATE-OS-1992-Q11$q$, 1992, 2, $q$Process Synchronization$q$, $q$Counting Semaphore Arithmetic$q$, 11, 'NAT', $q$A counting semaphore S is initialized to 7. Then 20 P (wait) operations and 15 V (signal) operations are executed on S in arbitrary order. What is the final value of S?$q$, $q${}$q$::jsonb, $q$2$q$, $q$The value of a counting semaphore S changes deterministically according to:
S_final = S_initial - (number of P operations) + (number of V operations)
S_final = 7 - 20 + 15 = 2.$q$, $q$1. Initial value: S = 7.
2. P operation decrements S: 20 * (-1) = -20.
3. V operation increments S: 15 * (+1) = +15.
4. S_final = 7 - 20 + 15 = 2.
5. Note: Since S_final > 0, no process remains blocked at the end.$q$, $q${}$q$::jsonb, ARRAY[$q$Counting Semaphore$q$,$q$Atomic Operations$q$,$q$State Update$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$S_final = S_initial - P_count + V_count$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$Order of execution does not alter net value if no process blocks permanently.$q$]::text[], 'Easy', $q$Assuming negative semaphore values without context.$q$, $q$Semaphore All GATE Questions | L 9$q$, 'Verified', $q$[]$q$::jsonb),
($q$GATE-OS-2007-Q22$q$, $q$GATE-OS-2007-Q22$q$, 2007, 2, $q$Process Synchronization$q$, $q$Peterson's Algorithm$q$, 22, 'MCQ', $q$Consider Peterson's algorithm for mutual exclusion between two processes P0 and P1. Which of the following properties are satisfied?
(I) Mutual Exclusion
(II) Progress
(III) Bounded Waiting$q$, $q${"A":"I only","B":"I and II only","C":"I and III only","D":"I, II, and III"}$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Peterson's solution uses two variables: `flag[2]` (indicating intent to enter critical section) and `turn` (resolving tie). It satisfies all three requirements of Critical Section Problem: Mutual Exclusion, Progress, and Bounded Waiting.$q$, $q$1. Mutual Exclusion: P0 enters CS only if `flag[1] == false` or `turn == 0`. P1 enters only if `flag[0] == false` or `turn == 1`. Since `turn` cannot equal both 0 and 1 simultaneously, both cannot be in CS together.
2. Progress: If one process doesn't want to enter (`flag[other] == false`), the interested process enters immediately without delay.
3. Bounded Waiting: A process waits at most one entry turn of the other process because `turn` is set to favor the waiting process upon re-entry.$q$, $q${"A":"Incomplete requirements check.","B":"Ignores bounded wait guarantee.","C":"Ignores progress guarantee.","D":"All three properties strictly satisfied."}$q$::jsonb, ARRAY[$q$Peterson's Solution$q$,$q$Critical Section Requirements$q$,$q$Mutual Exclusion$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$flag[i]=true; turn=j; while(flag[j] && turn==j);$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$Peterson's solution works for exactly 2 processes in software without hardware instructions.$q$]::text[], 'Medium', $q$Assuming Peterson's solution works for N processes without modification.$q$, $q$Process Synchronization GATE 2006-2012 | L 6$q$, 'Verified', $q$[]$q$::jsonb),
($q$GATE-OS-2015-Q31$q$, $q$GATE-OS-2015-Q31$q$, 2015, 2, $q$Process Synchronization$q$, $q$Producer-Consumer Bounded Buffer$q$, 31, 'MCQ', $q$In a bounded-buffer Producer-Consumer system with buffer capacity N, three semaphores are used: `mutex` initialized to 1, `full` initialized to 0, and `empty` initialized to N. What are the correct semaphore operations inside the Producer procedure before and after buffer insertion?$q$, $q${"A":"P(empty) then P(mutex); insertion; V(mutex) then V(full)","B":"P(mutex) then P(empty); insertion; V(full) then V(mutex)","C":"P(full) then P(mutex); insertion; V(mutex) then V(empty)","D":"P(empty) then P(full); insertion; V(mutex) then V(mutex)"}$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Producer must wait for an empty slot (`P(empty)`) BEFORE acquiring the binary mutex (`P(mutex)`). Reversing this (`P(mutex)` first) causes DEADLOCK if buffer is full because Producer holds `mutex` while waiting indefinitely for Consumer to execute `V(empty)`.$q$, $q$1. Empty slot check: Producer executes `P(empty)` first to reserve space.
2. Lock Critical Section: Producer executes `P(mutex)` to enter critical section.
3. Insert Item: `buffer[in] = item; in = (in+1)%N;`.
4. Unlock Critical Section: Producer executes `V(mutex)`.
5. Signal Item Ready: Producer executes `V(full)` to inform Consumer.$q$, $q${"A":"Correct deadlock-free operation sequence.","B":"Causes deadlock when buffer is full.","C":"Incorrect semaphore roles.","D":"Corrupts mutex state."}$q$::jsonb, ARRAY[$q$Producer-Consumer$q$,$q$Bounded Buffer$q$,$q$Deadlock Avoidance in Semaphores$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$P(empty) -> P(mutex) -> CS -> V(mutex) -> V(full)$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$Always execute resource counting semaphore before mutex lock!$q$]::text[], 'Hard', $q$Placing `P(mutex)` before `P(empty)`, causing buffer deadlock.$q$, $q$Process Synchronization GATE 2013-2021 | L 7$q$, 'Verified', $q$[]$q$::jsonb),
($q$GATE-OS-1996-Q04$q$, $q$GATE-OS-1996-Q04$q$, 1996, 2, $q$Process Synchronization$q$, $q$Binary Semaphore Race Condition$q$, 4, 'NAT', $q$Two processes P1 and P2 share a variable x (initialized to 0) and a binary semaphore S (initialized to 1). If P1 increments x 4 times and P2 decrements x 3 times inside CS, what is the value of x?$q$, $q${}$q$::jsonb, $q$1$q$, $q$Because access to x is strictly serialized within the critical section protected by semaphore S, race conditions are completely prevented.$q$, $q$1. Synchronization ensures mutual exclusion.
2. P1 adds +4 to x.
3. P2 subtracts -3 from x.
4. Net value of x = 0 + (4) - (3) = 1.$q$, $q${}$q$::jsonb, ARRAY[$q$Binary Semaphore$q$,$q$Critical Section$q$,$q$Race Condition Prevention$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$Net Value = Initial + Sum(Increments) - Sum(Decrements)$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$With binary semaphore lock, operations are strictly serializable.$q$]::text[], 'Medium', $q$Assuming concurrent interleaving within critical section.$q$, $q$Process Synchronization GATE 2000-2010 | L 5$q$, 'Verified', $q$[{"code":"TEMPLATE_FAMILY","severity":"info","message":"One of several questions with near-identical wording in this dataset (only numbers, year or format differ)."}]$q$::jsonb),
