($q$GATE-OS-2011-Q14$q$, $q$GATE-OS-2011-Q14$q$, 2011, 8, $q$Threads$q$, $q$User-Level vs Kernel-Level Threads$q$, 14, 'MCQ', $q$Which of the following statements is/are TRUE regarding User-Level Threads (ULT) and Kernel-Level Threads (KLT)?
(I) Blocking system call in one ULT blocks the entire process.
(II) Context switching between ULTs is faster than between KLTs.
(III) KLTs can take advantage of multi-processor systems natively.$q$, $q${"A":"I and II only","B":"II and III only","C":"I and III only","D":"I, II, and III"}$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$All three statements are strictly TRUE.
- (I) OS kernel only sees single process for ULTs; if one ULT makes blocking system call, kernel blocks whole process.
- (II) ULT context switch happens in user space without trap to kernel.
- (III) Kernel schedules KLTs on separate CPU cores concurrently.$q$, $q$1. Statement I: True. OS kernel is unaware of ULT existence. Blocking call blocks process thread container.
2. Statement II: True. ULT switch only updates user registers and stack pointer, avoiding kernel trap context switch overhead.
3. Statement III: True. Kernel thread scheduler assigns individual KLTs to physical processor cores.
4. Therefore, all I, II, and III are TRUE.$q$, $q${"A":"Omits multi-processor advantage.","B":"Omits process blocking downside.","C":"Omits context switch speed difference.","D":"All three properties correct."}$q$::jsonb, ARRAY[$q$User-Level Threads$q$,$q$Kernel-Level Threads$q$,$q$Multithreading Models$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$ULT Switch Time << KLT Switch Time$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$Remember ULTs cannot run on multiple CPU cores simultaneously.$q$]::text[], 'Easy', $q$Assuming ULTs can utilize multi-core CPUs natively.$q$, $q$Threads All GATE Questions | L 8$q$, 'Verified', $q$[]$q$::jsonb),
($q$GATE-OS-2014-Q02$q$, $q$GATE-OS-2014-Q02$q$, 2014, 8, $q$Threads$q$, $q$Thread Shared State & Stack$q$, 2, 'MSQ', $q$Which of the following resources is/are SHARED among threads of the same process?
(A) Heap Memory
(B) Global Variables
(C) Stack Pointer / Register Set
(D) Open File Descriptors$q$, $q${"A":"Heap Memory","B":"Global Variables","C":"Stack / Registers","D":"Open Files"}$q$::jsonb, $q$A,B,D$q$, $q$Threads belonging to the same process share code, global data, heap memory, and OS resources (open files, signals). However, EACH thread has its own PRIVATE stack, stack pointer, and register set.$q$, $q$1. Shared Resources: Heap, Global/Static variables, Code segment, File descriptors, Signal handlers.
2. Private Resources per Thread: Thread ID, Register state (PC, SP), Local variables (Stack).
3. Hence Stack / Registers are NOT shared.$q$, $q${}$q$::jsonb, ARRAY[$q$Thread Memory Model$q$,$q$Shared vs Private Resources$q$,$q$Concurrency$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$Thread State = Private Stack + Private Registers$q$]::text[], ARRAY[$q$Stack is private per thread for local function call frames.$q$]::text[], 'Easy', $q$Thinking stack space is shared across threads.$q$, $q$Threads All GATE Questions | L 8$q$, 'Verified', $q$[{"code":"TEMPLATE_FAMILY","severity":"info","message":"One of several questions with near-identical wording in this dataset (only numbers, year or format differ)."}]$q$::jsonb),
